      "document_name": "AI fictional teaching passage: a curator's account",
      "page": null,
      "text": "This passage and all its people, places and events are fictional, written by an AI assistant for a teaching exercise. The curator of the imagined North Quay community archive writes: We finally opened the exhibition together. Volunteers carried boxes from their homes, and children stopped before the wall of photographs. We chose captions short enough to read in a crowded room. The opening felt like a beginning, not a finished history. The passage is narrated by someone responsible for the exhibition. It does not identify who selected the photographs, list rejected contributions, or report every visitor's reaction.",
      "document_id": "fixture-doc-f65c9595535eb4ca8a298b9a"
    },
    {
      "id": "fiction_archive_method_en",
      "document_name": "AI-authored teaching note: perspective and interpretive limits",
      "page": null,
      "text": "A first-person account gives access to a situated viewpoint, not automatic knowledge of every participant's motives. Distinguish a narrator's explicit statement from an inference based on an image, omission or contrast. The curator's collective we can be read as an aspiration to shared ownership or as a phrase that leaves differences within the group unstated. The volunteer's applause and unopened letter can support readings of tentative belonging, unresolved exclusion, or a mixture, but the text does not establish censorship or identify the letter's contents. A defensible interpretation compares details from both passages, acknowledges a plausible competing reading, and explains the limits of its claim. Different interpretations can meet these standards without using identical wording or agreeing on a single hidden intention.",
      "document_id": "fixture-doc-0ea144811889ab49827c6963"
    }
  ]
}

